    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Guide</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Do Boat Tours in Madeira Include Hotel Pickup?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>31 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">It depends on the operator, and Chifbay&#x27;s private boat trips do not include one: every trip starts and ends on the pontoon at Marina do Funchal, in the middle of the seafront. From most Funchal hotels that is a walk, a short taxi ride or a city bus away.</p>

      <h2>Why does a private boat trip start at the marina?</h2><p>Because the trip is the boat. What you book is the whole boat for your group, the skippers and everything on board, starting the moment you step aboard and ending when you step off. A transfer is not part of that price.</p><p>Your Chifbay skipper meets you on the pontoon, and the time on your booking is the time the boat leaves, not a collection window. That makes the start simple: one place, one time, and nobody waiting in a van outside another hotel.</p><p>The flip side is that getting there is yours to plan. Marina do Funchal sits in the middle of the seafront, so that is rarely hard.</p><h2>How long does it take to reach the marina?</h2><p>From central Funchal, minutes. From elsewhere on the island, it depends on the road. These are rough guides on a normal day. Add time at busy hours, and give yourself the 15 minutes before departure the skipper asks for on top.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Where you are staying</th><th>Best way in</th><th>Roughly how long</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Old town, Zona Velha</td><td>Walk along the seafront</td><td>About 15 minutes, around 1 km</td></tr><tr><td>City centre, near the cathedral</td><td>Walk</td><td>A few minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Lido and Estrada Monumental hotels</td><td>Walk, taxi or city bus</td><td>About 3 km, the best part of 40 minutes on foot</td></tr><tr><td>Caniço, to the east</td><td>Taxi or car</td><td>About 10 km, often 10 to 15 minutes by road</td></tr><tr><td>Calheta, in the west</td><td>Car or taxi</td><td>About 31 km, around half an hour by road</td></tr><tr><td>Madeira airport</td><td>Taxi, car or airport bus</td><td>About 18 to 22 km, 20 to 30 minutes by road</td></tr><tr><td>Cruise ship at the Pontinha pier</td><td>Walk along the waterfront</td><td>About 15 to 25 minutes, depending on the berth</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Is walking a good idea?</h2><p>From the old town and the centre, yes, easily. The seafront is the natural way in, with the sea on one side and the marina's masts ahead.</p><p>From the Lido it is a real walk rather than a stroll. It is pleasant on a cool morning before a 10:00 Day Trip. It is less pleasant on a hot July afternoon, or on the way back after three hours of sun and swimming. A good compromise is to walk there and take a taxi home.</p><p>Our page on the <a href="/posts/funchal-marina-meeting-point-for-boat-trips">Funchal marina meeting point</a> has the walking routes step by step.</p><h2>What about taxis and ride apps?</h2><p>Taxis are the easiest door to pontoon option for most hotels. Funchal's taxis are yellow, there are ranks in the centre and along the seafront, and asking for "Marina do Funchal" is enough. Hotel receptions will usually call one for you.</p><p>Ride apps work on the island too. Bolt has run in Madeira for years, and Uber came back in December 2024 after leaving in 2021. The regional government keeps a tight limit on licences for these cars, though, so at busy times the wait can be longer than in a big city. Order early, or ask your hotel for a taxi instead.</p><h2>Can you take the bus?</h2><p>Yes, and the seafront is the hub. Since July 2024 Madeira's buses have run as one network under the SIGA name. Horários do Funchal runs the city routes, and Rodoeste covers the west of the island, including Câmara de Lobos, Ribeira Brava, Ponta do Sol and Calheta. An airport bus links the airport with Funchal.</p><p>Buses are cheap and fine for getting into town, with one honest warning. Timetables outside the main routes can be thin, and a missed bus may mean a long wait. For a trip with a fixed departure, catch one bus earlier than you think you need. Check times on the SIGA website before the day, and see our guide to <a href="/posts/getting-around-madeira-car-bus-taxi-boat-guide">getting around Madeira</a> for the wider picture.</p><h2>What if you are staying outside Funchal?</h2><p>Then the drive is part of the plan. From Caniço or Garajau to the east, the road into Funchal is short. From Calheta, Ponta do Sol or the north coast, allow more, and remember that a Sunset Trip always ends after the sun has gone down, so someone will be driving back in the dark.</p><p>Parking near the seafront fills quickly, especially in summer and on cruise days. If you drive, arrive early enough to look for a space without watching the clock. If you would rather not drive at all, a taxi there and back is often simpler than it sounds.</p><p>If you are staying far out west and wonder whether a trip could start somewhere else, ask us on WhatsApp. The set trips always leave from Marina do Funchal, so ask before you book rather than after.</p><h2>And if you arrive on a cruise ship?</h2><p>The marina is almost next door. Ships in Funchal tie up at the Pontinha pier, and the walk to the marina runs along the waterfront, flat most of the way. Allow 15 to 25 minutes from the gangway, more if your ship is at the far end of the pier. Our page for <a href="/posts/cruise-passengers-private-boat-trip-funchal-back-on-time">cruise passengers on a private boat trip</a> covers timings and the ship's all-aboard time.</p><h2>When is the lack of pickup a real problem?</h2><p>It depends on who is in your group. For most people, getting to the marina is the easiest part of the day. It can be harder if someone walks with difficulty, if you are staying far from Funchal with no car, or if it is your arrival day and you are still finding your feet.</p><p>In those cases, message us on WhatsApp before you book and explain the situation. We will tell you honestly whether the pontoon and the boat suit your group. For everything else about the day, the <a href="/practical">practical information page</a> has the meeting point, times and what to bring, and you can compare both trips on the <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour in Madeira</a> page before you choose.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Where does the Chifbay boat leave from?</h3><p>From the pontoon at Marina do Funchal, right in the centre of town by the sea.</p><h3>Can the skipper collect us from Calheta or Caniço?</h3><p>The set trips always start and end at Marina do Funchal. Ask on WhatsApp before booking about anything else.</p><h3>How do I get back to my hotel after the trip?</h3><p>Walk if you are in central Funchal, or take a taxi from a rank in the centre. Ride apps work too, but cars can take a while at busy times.</p><h3>Is it a long walk from a cruise ship to the boat?</h3><p>Roughly 15 to 25 minutes on foot from the Pontinha pier, along the waterfront, depending on where the ship berths.</p>

      <blockquote>No van, no pickup window: one pontoon, one time, in the middle of Funchal&#x27;s seafront.</blockquote>
    </article>
